1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Disclosed by the B039 lane 2026-08-30 as a deliberate decision, not found in
review. Filed so the decision is tracked rather than rediscovered.

## Defect

`Backend` holds two independent locks:

    src/server/mod.rs:124   type_index:       Arc<RwLock<Option<Arc<TypeIndex>>>>
    src/server/mod.rs:133   type_db_versions: Arc<RwLock<Option<TypeDbVersions>>>

They are written non-atomically (`src/server/mod.rs:1236` and `:1247`), and
every reader takes one lock at a time. So a reader can observe a NEW index
paired with STALE stamps, and two concurrent regenerations can interleave to
leave the pair permanently describing different loads.

## Severity: low, and be honest about why

The consequence is a wrong REPORT, not wrong analysis — diagnostics still come
from whatever index is loaded. It needs concurrent `openplanet.regenerateTypeDb`
invocations, which are user-triggered from the palette, so it takes deliberate
double-firing to hit.

But it is worth fixing eventually because the report is the entire user-visible
point of B039's change. `before`/`after`/`changed` exists so a user can tell
whether regenerating actually did anything. A field that can silently describe
a different load than the one in memory undermines the one thing it is for.

## Work

Hold the stamps and the index under a SINGLE lock so they cannot disagree —
e.g. one `RwLock<Option<LoadedTypeDb>>` where `LoadedTypeDb { index, versions }`.

Note the cost honestly: `type_index` is read in roughly ten places
(`mod.rs` :770, :837, :956, :1518 and others), so this is a mechanical refactor
of every read site, not a two-line change. That is why the B039 lane correctly
declined to do it inline.

Do NOT 'fix' this by acquiring both write locks in a fixed order. That
serialises writers but readers still take one lock at a time, so a reader can
still observe a torn pair. Only a single lock actually closes it.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

Red: drive two concurrent `regenerateTypeDb` executions against a server whose
DB changes between them, and assert the reported `after` stamps equal the
stamps of the index actually loaded. Deterministic reproduction will likely
need an injected pause between the two writes.

Variants:
- concurrent regenerations -> reported stamps always describe the loaded index
- a reader mid-regeneration never sees index and stamps from different loads
- single-threaded behaviour completely unchanged (the B039 tests still pass)
- a FAILED regeneration leaves index and stamps consistent with each other

